Title: Asymmetric Decentralisation in South Africa: Implications for Municipal Governance
Abstract: This paper examines how asymmetric decentralisation shapes the performance of South Africa’s municipal sphere. Although all municipalities occupy the same constitutional tier, they face differentiated expenditure mandates, revenue-raising powers, and financing arrangements. Using a qualitative case study drawing from audit reports, national financial datasets, and intergovernmental policy documents, the paper shows that metropolitan municipalities benefit from broader own-revenue bases and greater administrative capacity, while many local and district municipalities remain heavily transfer-dependent and fiscally constrained. These structural asymmetries produce persistent vertical and horizontal fiscal imbalances, weaker capital execution and maintenance effort, and ultimately uneven service delivery outcomes. The study argues for capacity-linked functional assignments, strengthened equalisation instruments, clearer mandate definitions, and improved conditional grant design to ensure that finance follows function and that asymmetric decentralisation does not reinforce territorial inequality.

Keywords: asymmetric decentralisation, fiscal federalism, local government, municipal finance, equalisation, service delivery, South Africa

